WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.167 --> 00:00:03.870 align:start size:84% position:16%
- And we saw the CDC
officials and NIH

00:00:03.870 --> 00:00:07.007
and Fauci specifically
attack me along with

00:00:07.007 --> 00:00:09.843
Elizabeth Warren and Rachel
Maddow, night after night.

00:00:09.843 --> 00:00:13.513 align:start size:94% position:6%
But we also, you know,
recognized too that people

00:00:13.513 --> 00:00:17.017 align:start size:97% position:3%
in my state weren't
necessarily supportive either.

00:00:17.017 --> 00:00:19.753
You know, there was times
where it was hard to find

00:00:19.753 --> 00:00:22.155
a lot of encouragement, even
from people who lived here,

00:00:22.155 --> 00:00:24.391 align:start size:88% position:13%
that my Lieutenant
Governor tells the story

00:00:24.391 --> 00:00:27.627
that he would get phone calls
from even our best supporters

00:00:27.627 --> 00:00:30.830 align:start size:97% position:3%
that would say, hey, you gotta
get the governor in line.

00:00:30.830 --> 00:00:33.600
(relaxing music)

00:00:37.737 --> 00:00:39.439 line:0%
- I'm Dave Rubin and
joining me today is the

00:00:39.439 --> 00:00:42.075 line:0%
Governor of the great
state of South Dakota,

00:00:42.075 --> 00:00:44.544 line:0%
as well as author
of the new book,

00:00:44.544 --> 00:00:47.947 align:start size:97% position:3%
Not My First Rodeo:
Lessons from the Heartland.

00:00:47.947 --> 00:00:50.450 align:start size:97% position:3%
Governor Kristi Noem.
Welcome to the Rubin Report.

00:00:50.450 --> 00:00:52.585
- Oh, thank you. I'm
thrilled to be here.

00:00:52.585 --> 00:00:54.020
I appreciate the invitation.

00:00:54.020 --> 00:00:56.122 align:start size:84% position:16%
- I am glad to
finally chat with you.

00:00:56.122 --> 00:00:58.858 align:start size:97% position:3%
Because I always tell
people I have a short list.

00:00:58.858 --> 00:01:00.326
It's a very short list.

00:01:00.326 --> 00:01:03.396 align:start size:97% position:3%
I have it in front of me
right now of sane politicians

00:01:03.396 --> 00:01:05.465
and you're on the list,

00:01:05.465 --> 00:01:07.000 align:start size:84% position:16%
so congratulations.
- Oh, good.

00:01:07.000 --> 00:01:07.967
Oh, it's a good day.

00:01:07.967 --> 00:01:09.769
That's wonderful to hear.

00:01:09.769 --> 00:01:11.104
I'll put it on my resume.

00:01:11.104 --> 00:01:12.338
- There you go.

00:01:12.338 --> 00:01:15.141
So I want to dive into
a bit about the book

00:01:15.141 --> 00:01:18.311
and a bit about hot topics
of the day and a bunch more.

00:01:18.311 --> 00:01:20.847
But in the book you kind
of go into your history,

00:01:20.847 --> 00:01:23.416 align:start size:94% position:6%
your childhood, and really
how it sets up a lot

00:01:23.416 --> 00:01:24.584
of your political beliefs.

00:01:24.584 --> 00:01:26.086 align:start size:84% position:16%
So I thought we could
just start there

00:01:26.086 --> 00:01:27.520
maybe for some of the people

00:01:27.520 --> 00:01:31.024
that don't know too
much about your bio.

00:01:31.024 --> 00:01:33.393 line:0%
- Well, I think a lot of
people would look at me

00:01:33.393 --> 00:01:36.296 line:0%
writing a book and think
it's a book about politics

00:01:36.296 --> 00:01:37.230 line:0%
and it's really not.

00:01:37.230 --> 00:01:39.632 line:0%
It's about my life lived so far.

00:01:39.632 --> 00:01:42.502
Most people heard my name for
the first time during COVID

00:01:42.502 --> 00:01:45.138 align:start size:81% position:19%
and didn't know
anything about me.

00:01:45.138 --> 00:01:46.940 align:start size:84% position:16%
Maybe made some
assumptions based on

00:01:46.940 --> 00:01:48.408
the decisions that I was making.

00:01:48.408 --> 00:01:50.176 align:start size:94% position:6%
And I think it's
important that for people

00:01:50.176 --> 00:01:53.713 align:start size:84% position:16%
to understand the
decisions that I make,

00:01:53.713 --> 00:01:55.582
they have to know a
little bit about me.

00:01:55.582 --> 00:01:57.984 align:start size:94% position:6%
The way I grew up, I
think was pretty special.

00:01:57.984 --> 00:02:00.954 align:start size:97% position:3%
There's a lot of life lessons
I learned from my family,

00:02:00.954 --> 00:02:02.822
my faith and the ranch.

00:02:02.822 --> 00:02:06.126 align:start size:84% position:16%
And I think it is
some of those lessons,

00:02:06.126 --> 00:02:09.395 align:start size:97% position:3%
that helped South Dakota and
myself do what we've done

00:02:09.395 --> 00:02:10.597
the last couple of years.

00:02:10.597 --> 00:02:13.833 align:start size:84% position:16%
That I believe really
is an inspiration

00:02:13.833 --> 00:02:15.068
to the rest of the country.

00:02:15.068 --> 00:02:17.770
Here in South Dakota we
have embraced freedom,

00:02:17.770 --> 00:02:19.439
unlike any other state.

00:02:19.439 --> 00:02:21.307
And it really is
benefiting not only

00:02:21.307 --> 00:02:22.575
the people who live here,

00:02:22.575 --> 00:02:24.978
but also our economy
and our way of life.

00:02:24.978 --> 00:02:26.246
- Yeah, it really has.

00:02:26.246 --> 00:02:28.181
I mentioned to you right
before we started that,

00:02:28.181 --> 00:02:30.016 align:start size:84% position:16%
although I didn't
quite get to meet you.

00:02:30.016 --> 00:02:31.818
We were separated
by about five feet.

00:02:31.818 --> 00:02:34.220
We walked past each
other at Freedom Fest

00:02:34.220 --> 00:02:35.488
after you had spoke.

00:02:35.488 --> 00:02:37.457
And then I went to Mount
Rushmore that very day.

00:02:37.457 --> 00:02:38.958
It was the first time
I had ever been there

00:02:38.958 --> 00:02:40.493
and just the spirit of America.

00:02:40.493 --> 00:02:43.496 align:start size:94% position:6%
And it was still the
middle of COVID I suppose,

00:02:43.496 --> 00:02:44.998
but just the amount
of people that were

00:02:44.998 --> 00:02:48.735
out in street festivals, out
and about happy, you know,

00:02:48.735 --> 00:02:50.003
some wearing masks, some not.

00:02:50.003 --> 00:02:51.538
Making decisions for themselves.

00:02:51.538 --> 00:02:53.139
It was so refreshing.

00:02:53.139 --> 00:02:54.741
But can you go into
a little bit of that,

00:02:54.741 --> 00:02:56.242
growing up in the Heartland,

00:02:56.242 --> 00:02:58.878 align:start size:97% position:3%
and what some of that family
stuff that you learned,

00:02:58.878 --> 00:03:00.980
maybe how that translated
into some of the politics

00:03:00.980 --> 00:03:02.549
that we'll get to?

00:03:02.549 --> 00:03:05.251 align:start size:88% position:13%
- Well, my dad was a
cowboy and he was tough.

00:03:05.251 --> 00:03:08.254
All we did was work and if we
didn't work, we were hunting.

00:03:08.254 --> 00:03:10.056
In fact, even our
family vacations

00:03:10.056 --> 00:03:12.559 align:start size:88% position:13%
were loading the horses
up in the trailer

00:03:12.559 --> 00:03:14.494 align:start size:88% position:13%
and headed to the
mountains for two weeks,

00:03:14.494 --> 00:03:17.297
on elk hunts or big game hunts.

00:03:17.297 --> 00:03:19.465 align:start size:94% position:6%
And so it was a very
different childhood maybe,

00:03:19.465 --> 00:03:21.000 align:start size:81% position:19%
than typically
what American kids

00:03:21.000 --> 00:03:23.369
get the chance to
experience today.

00:03:23.369 --> 00:03:25.405
But I also learned a
lot from having a dad

00:03:25.405 --> 00:03:29.075 align:start size:88% position:13%
that gave us incredibly
hard things to do.

00:03:29.075 --> 00:03:30.910
You know, many times growing up,

00:03:30.910 --> 00:03:33.646
I thought is this guy
trying to kill us?

00:03:33.646 --> 00:03:36.282 align:start size:81% position:19%
Cause he just gave
us challenges.

00:03:36.282 --> 00:03:38.318 align:start size:84% position:16%
But I realized
that by doing that,

00:03:38.318 --> 00:03:40.587
he made us into problem solvers.

00:03:40.587 --> 00:03:42.789 align:start size:94% position:6%
And when we did something
that was very hard

00:03:42.789 --> 00:03:44.757
that we didn't think
we could accomplish,

00:03:44.757 --> 00:03:48.061 align:start size:97% position:3%
it gave us confidence to
tackle the next biggest thing.

00:03:48.061 --> 00:03:50.129
So, a lot of the
stories in the book

00:03:50.129 --> 00:03:53.433 align:start size:88% position:13%
are working with
cattle or hunting trips

00:03:53.433 --> 00:03:56.035
or lessons learned
from my mom and dad.

00:03:56.035 --> 00:03:58.404
But they also address
kind of a way of life,

00:03:58.404 --> 00:04:00.773
that's kind of about
the American West,

00:04:00.773 --> 00:04:02.976 align:start size:97% position:3%
that I think people are still
very curious about today.

00:04:02.976 --> 00:04:06.579
And our politics were not
talked about in our home.

00:04:06.579 --> 00:04:08.982 align:start size:84% position:16%
We weren't a political
family at all.

00:04:08.982 --> 00:04:11.784
Instead they were
lived, they were in

00:04:11.784 --> 00:04:13.853
how we lived our way of life.

00:04:13.853 --> 00:04:16.656 align:start position:0%
In fact, I remember I was
telling somebody the other day,

00:04:16.656 --> 00:04:20.326
when South Dakota passed the
law that required seat belts.

00:04:20.326 --> 00:04:25.331 align:start position:0%
I remember being young and
watching my dad take a jackknife

00:04:26.799 --> 00:04:28.167
and then cut the seat belts
out of his brand new pickup.

00:04:28.167 --> 00:04:31.004
And I asked him why
he was doing that.

00:04:31.004 --> 00:04:33.806
And he told me because the
state had just passed a law

00:04:33.806 --> 00:04:36.376 align:start size:94% position:6%
that mandated it and it
wasn't that he was against

00:04:36.376 --> 00:04:38.778
wearing his seatbelt,
he wore his seatbelt.

00:04:38.778 --> 00:04:40.580 align:start size:84% position:16%
It was that he was
against the government

00:04:40.580 --> 00:04:42.682
telling him he had to.

00:04:42.682 --> 00:04:44.751
And it was lessons like that.

00:04:44.751 --> 00:04:47.620 align:start size:88% position:13%
That really made me
think about government,

00:04:47.620 --> 00:04:50.423 align:start size:88% position:13%
its role in your life
in a very different way,

00:04:50.423 --> 00:04:52.558 align:start size:97% position:3%
than I think a lot of people
get the chance to today,

00:04:52.558 --> 00:04:53.760
when they just turn on the news

00:04:53.760 --> 00:04:56.195
and get their opinions that way.

00:04:56.195 --> 00:04:59.299
- So from a family that wasn't
necessarily talking politics,

00:04:59.299 --> 00:05:01.167
but sort of living
it as you describe.

00:05:01.167 --> 00:05:05.505 align:start size:88% position:13%
What possessed you to
get into this crazy game

00:05:05.505 --> 00:05:07.473
known as politics of today.

00:05:08.641 --> 00:05:10.443
- You know, I would
say it was a tragedy.

00:05:10.443 --> 00:05:13.579
We'd lost my dad in an
accident on our farm,

00:05:13.579 --> 00:05:17.717 align:start size:94% position:6%
when he was 49 years old,
I was 22 at the time.

00:05:17.717 --> 00:05:19.786
I was married, I'd gotten
married when I was 20.

00:05:19.786 --> 00:05:21.688
But I was still going to college

00:05:21.688 --> 00:05:23.589
and it just turned our
whole lives upside down.

00:05:23.589 --> 00:05:26.025
He was kind of Superman.

00:05:26.025 --> 00:05:29.195
You know, we had a very
large farming operation

00:05:29.195 --> 00:05:31.297
and ranching operation,

00:05:31.297 --> 00:05:32.665
probably one of the
largest in the state.

00:05:32.665 --> 00:05:34.500
Many other businesses.

00:05:34.500 --> 00:05:37.904 align:start size:84% position:16%
And when he was
gone, we had no idea

00:05:37.904 --> 00:05:39.906
how we were gonna keep
the operation going.

00:05:39.906 --> 00:05:43.509
In fact, I became the
general manager at 22

00:05:43.509 --> 00:05:46.512
and you know, had a lot
of people working for us,

00:05:46.512 --> 00:05:49.282 align:start size:97% position:3%
relying on us, trying not
to lose our family business.

00:05:49.282 --> 00:05:51.617
When I got a letter in
the mail from the IRS

00:05:51.617 --> 00:05:54.253
that said we owed death taxes

00:05:54.253 --> 00:05:57.523 align:start size:88% position:13%
and I hadn't heard of
death taxes before that.

00:05:57.523 --> 00:06:01.027 align:start size:97% position:3%
But all of a sudden,
because we had this tragedy

00:06:01.027 --> 00:06:02.929
and our whole lives have
been turned upside down.

00:06:02.929 --> 00:06:05.832 align:start position:0%
Now we owed hundreds and
hundreds of thousands of dollars

00:06:05.832 --> 00:06:09.168
to the federal government in
taxes and it made me angry.

00:06:09.168 --> 00:06:10.770
So that's what got me involved.

00:06:10.770 --> 00:06:12.872 align:start size:88% position:13%
Is I just started
showing up at meetings.

00:06:12.872 --> 00:06:14.874
I was passionate
about tax reform.

00:06:16.342 --> 00:06:19.712
At that time our Senator from
South Dakota was Tom Daschle.

00:06:19.712 --> 00:06:23.449 align:start size:97% position:3%
He was the US Senate
Majority Leader at the time.

00:06:23.449 --> 00:06:26.018
And I showed up at
all of his meetings

00:06:26.018 --> 00:06:27.653
and started talking policy

00:06:27.653 --> 00:06:31.791
and remembering that my dad
always told us growing up,

00:06:31.791 --> 00:06:34.761
Kristi, we don't complain
about things, we fix 'em.

00:06:35.962 --> 00:06:38.197
But it took me 10 years
to pay off those taxes.

00:06:38.197 --> 00:06:40.299
So during that time I
was pretty passionate

00:06:40.299 --> 00:06:42.969
about getting rid
of that unfair tax

00:06:42.969 --> 00:06:45.605
that hits a family
at a time of tragedy.

00:06:45.605 --> 00:06:48.474
- Yeah. So as you
mentioned earlier,

00:06:48.474 --> 00:06:50.777
what sort of put you
on the map nationally

00:06:50.777 --> 00:06:54.614 align:start position:0%
was the COVID situation and
that you didn't go for lockdowns

00:06:54.614 --> 00:06:57.016 align:start size:88% position:13%
and you fought the
system and all of that.

00:06:57.016 --> 00:07:00.219
As you may know, I lived in
California for most of COVID.

00:07:00.219 --> 00:07:02.288 align:start size:84% position:16%
I now live in the
free state of Florida.

00:07:02.288 --> 00:07:04.190 align:start size:84% position:16%
And as I keep
telling my audience,

00:07:04.190 --> 00:07:05.658
it's like living
in another country,

00:07:05.658 --> 00:07:08.394
I genuinely feel that
I left one country

00:07:08.394 --> 00:07:11.063
and moved to another, which
is the beauty of federalism.

00:07:11.063 --> 00:07:13.633 align:start size:97% position:3%
Can you talk a little bit
about those first couple weeks

00:07:13.633 --> 00:07:16.068 align:start size:84% position:16%
of COVID, the pressure
you were under,

00:07:16.068 --> 00:07:19.405
and how you made your
decisions, you know,

00:07:19.405 --> 00:07:21.541 align:start size:88% position:13%
who you were taking
advice from, et cetera?

00:07:22.942 --> 00:07:26.145
- Well, I would say it was,
it was immense pressure.

00:07:26.145 --> 00:07:28.548
You know, we were
told right away

00:07:28.548 --> 00:07:30.983
before the virus even came
to the state of South Dakota

00:07:30.983 --> 00:07:34.320 align:start size:84% position:16%
that I would have over
300,000 people die,

00:07:34.320 --> 00:07:36.489
from this virus that was coming

00:07:36.489 --> 00:07:38.591
and that I needed to
do something about it.

00:07:38.591 --> 00:07:39.859
To slow down the spread

00:07:39.859 --> 00:07:41.994
and protect people
who were vulnerable.

00:07:41.994 --> 00:07:43.996 align:start size:94% position:6%
So I think I did what
every other governor did,

00:07:43.996 --> 00:07:46.666
talked to a lot of health
officials and experts.

00:07:46.666 --> 00:07:49.535 align:start size:94% position:6%
Studied the virus,
learned from other states

00:07:49.535 --> 00:07:50.870
when they were first affected.

00:07:50.870 --> 00:07:53.473 align:start size:84% position:16%
What I did differently
was, I believe,

00:07:53.473 --> 00:07:56.442
I spent a lot more time
with my general counsel,

00:07:56.442 --> 00:07:58.211 align:start size:94% position:6%
a lot of time with
constitutional attorneys.

00:07:58.211 --> 00:08:01.914
I really wanted to understand
what my job was as governor

00:08:01.914 --> 00:08:04.851 align:start size:97% position:3%
and what my job wasn't, what
authority I did not have,

00:08:04.851 --> 00:08:06.652 align:start size:84% position:16%
cause I just believe
that when leaders

00:08:06.652 --> 00:08:08.321
overstep their authority,

00:08:08.321 --> 00:08:10.456
especially in a time
of crisis like that,

00:08:10.456 --> 00:08:11.657
that that's really when we break

00:08:11.657 --> 00:08:12.925
the foundation of this country.

00:08:12.925 --> 00:08:15.127
I knew for a fact,

00:08:15.127 --> 00:08:17.497
I did not want to be the
governor who did that.

00:08:17.497 --> 00:08:20.500
So South Dakota did end
up being the only state

00:08:20.500 --> 00:08:23.002
that never once
closed a business

00:08:23.002 --> 00:08:26.839 align:start position:0%
or mandated anything while other
states were closing beaches

00:08:26.839 --> 00:08:28.374
and closing businesses

00:08:28.374 --> 00:08:31.077 align:start size:88% position:13%
and stopping big
meetings and gatherings.

00:08:31.077 --> 00:08:33.212 align:start size:94% position:6%
South Dakota did
something very different.

00:08:33.212 --> 00:08:36.949
In fact, I was shocked and
surprised how people were

00:08:36.949 --> 00:08:40.887 align:start position:0%
rolling over and giving up
their freedoms, in many states.

00:08:40.887 --> 00:08:43.589
So we went on the
offense, you know,

00:08:43.589 --> 00:08:47.426
we ran a national advertising
campaign right away in 2020,

00:08:47.426 --> 00:08:50.696 align:start size:94% position:6%
inviting everybody to come
to South Dakota while,

00:08:50.696 --> 00:08:51.931
while your state's locked down,

00:08:51.931 --> 00:08:54.700 align:start size:81% position:19%
come and spend
time here be free.

00:08:54.700 --> 00:08:58.104 align:start size:94% position:6%
And hundreds of
thousands of people came,

00:08:58.104 --> 00:09:00.006 align:start size:84% position:16%
they came and spent
some time here.

00:09:00.006 --> 00:09:02.041
And then many of those
people went back home,

00:09:02.041 --> 00:09:03.843 align:start size:88% position:13%
packed up their
families and moved here.

00:09:03.843 --> 00:09:05.645 align:start size:84% position:16%
So it's been an
incredible testimony,

00:09:05.645 --> 00:09:08.381
to what happened
in that pandemic.

00:09:08.381 --> 00:09:11.450 align:start size:88% position:13%
- I think the very day,
that crazy video of

00:09:11.450 --> 00:09:15.121
the cop chasing down the
guy in Malibu on the beach,

00:09:15.121 --> 00:09:16.989 align:start size:94% position:6%
remember that viral video.
- Yes I do.

00:09:16.989 --> 00:09:18.457
Absolutely.

00:09:18.457 --> 00:09:20.192 align:start size:97% position:3%
I remember that day and I
lived not too far from there.

00:09:20.192 --> 00:09:22.261
I saw you on Fox
saying you know,

00:09:22.261 --> 00:09:25.031 align:start size:94% position:6%
we're gonna stay open
and really thinking, man,

00:09:25.031 --> 00:09:27.667 align:start size:88% position:13%
this is what's happening
in America right now.

00:09:27.667 --> 00:09:29.969
It's just many, many
different countries.

00:09:29.969 --> 00:09:32.972
So were you talking to
other governors also?

00:09:32.972 --> 00:09:35.474
I mean, you know, some
like DeSantis obviously

00:09:35.474 --> 00:09:38.010 align:start size:97% position:3%
were doing something a lot
closer to what you were doing,

00:09:38.010 --> 00:09:40.780
but you know, Gavin Newsom
who was my governor in Cali

00:09:40.780 --> 00:09:42.648 align:start size:94% position:6%
was obviously doing
something very different.

00:09:42.648 --> 00:09:45.051
Hochul up in or at the time
it was Cuomo in New York,

00:09:45.051 --> 00:09:47.653 align:start size:97% position:3%
but now Hochul has continued
a lot of that stuff.

00:09:48.688 --> 00:09:50.089
- Well, we talked a lot.

00:09:50.089 --> 00:09:51.991
Not so much with
Democrat governors.

00:09:51.991 --> 00:09:54.160
You know, there would be
different conference calls

00:09:54.160 --> 00:09:56.095
through the National
Governor's Association

00:09:56.095 --> 00:09:57.730
that were hosted
and facilitated.

00:09:57.730 --> 00:10:01.500
But I learned quickly that
those weren't beneficial,

00:10:01.500 --> 00:10:03.636
that we weren't getting any
new information from them.

00:10:03.636 --> 00:10:05.338
And that we needed to prioritize

00:10:05.338 --> 00:10:07.607
where my time was being spent.

00:10:07.607 --> 00:10:09.875
We did have conference
calls with governors

00:10:09.875 --> 00:10:12.345
who were keeping
their states open.

00:10:12.345 --> 00:10:15.915 align:start size:94% position:6%
And you know, we just
organized that ourselves.

00:10:15.915 --> 00:10:18.517 align:start size:88% position:13% line:0%
We were all Republicans,
but we had weekly,

00:10:18.517 --> 00:10:21.520 align:start size:88% position:13% line:0%
sometimes biweekly phone
calls with learning

00:10:21.520 --> 00:10:23.656 line:0%
what we could learn about
the virus from each other.

00:10:23.656 --> 00:10:25.625
It was a governor's only call.

00:10:25.625 --> 00:10:27.593 align:start size:88% position:13%
Also learning what
other people were doing,

00:10:27.593 --> 00:10:30.396
what measures they were taking,
what they were recommending.

00:10:30.396 --> 00:10:33.299 align:start size:94% position:6%
But we also made sure
that that call only stayed

00:10:33.299 --> 00:10:35.835
for the governors that
kept their states open

00:10:35.835 --> 00:10:39.472
and every week, less and less
governors were on the call.

00:10:39.472 --> 00:10:43.275
It was kind of interesting
how candid we were, you know,

00:10:43.275 --> 00:10:45.978
so we'd get on that call
and somebody would say,

00:10:45.978 --> 00:10:48.247
hey guys, I gotta let you know,

00:10:48.247 --> 00:10:50.483 align:start size:84% position:16%
this is my last phone
call I'll be on.

00:10:50.483 --> 00:10:52.918
My entire team is telling me

00:10:52.918 --> 00:10:54.520 align:start size:84% position:16%
that I've gotta
shut down businesses

00:10:54.520 --> 00:10:55.921
cause the spread's
getting too bad.

00:10:55.921 --> 00:10:58.257
So I'm just here to let
you know that tomorrow,

00:10:58.257 --> 00:11:01.227 align:start size:97% position:3%
I'm putting out a press
release and closing the state

00:11:01.227 --> 00:11:03.729 align:start size:84% position:16%
and I won't be on
these calls anymore.

00:11:03.729 --> 00:11:07.199
And we would say, okay, and
then there was less and less.

00:11:07.199 --> 00:11:10.569
So there was times where
it got down to 12 of us

00:11:10.569 --> 00:11:13.139
and then a week later
there was eight of us.

00:11:13.139 --> 00:11:15.608
And then there was less of us

00:11:15.608 --> 00:11:18.577
and down until I was
the only one left.

00:11:18.577 --> 00:11:23.549 align:start size:97% position:3%
So it was, it was interesting
times, but I also,

00:11:23.549 --> 00:11:25.351 align:start size:84% position:16%
many of those
Republican governors

00:11:25.351 --> 00:11:28.487 align:start size:97% position:3%
maybe didn't have the
situation that I had either.

00:11:28.487 --> 00:11:31.190 align:start size:94% position:6%
You know, I had an
incredible executive team

00:11:31.190 --> 00:11:33.359 align:start size:94% position:6%
that when I made a
decision, they said okay,

00:11:33.359 --> 00:11:34.894
that's the decision
and they did it.

00:11:34.894 --> 00:11:39.865 align:start size:97% position:3%
And my hospital administrators
were very involved.

00:11:39.865 --> 00:11:41.901
We talked many times a day,

00:11:41.901 --> 00:11:44.470
when we made decisions,
it was based on science.

00:11:44.470 --> 00:11:47.306
It was not based on what
was playing on the news.

00:11:47.306 --> 00:11:48.808
That was very helpful.

00:11:48.808 --> 00:11:50.609 align:start size:97% position:3%
They were common sense,
reasonable people who wanted

00:11:50.609 --> 00:11:53.412
to take care of each other and
the state partnered with them

00:11:53.412 --> 00:11:55.614
in ways that I don't think
happened in other states.

00:11:55.614 --> 00:11:59.218
Our National Guard and other
entities were heavily involved

00:11:59.218 --> 00:12:03.589
in helping them build up
capacity and staffing,

00:12:03.589 --> 00:12:05.958
very innovative in
how we approached it,

00:12:05.958 --> 00:12:08.160
that I think allowed
us to do what we did.

00:12:08.160 --> 00:12:10.463
- What kind of pushback were
you getting behind the scenes,

00:12:10.463 --> 00:12:12.364 align:start size:84% position:16%
even if you had
support in the state?

00:12:12.364 --> 00:12:13.866
I would imagine, you know,

00:12:13.866 --> 00:12:16.202
there had to be some calls
from maybe people at the NIH

00:12:16.202 --> 00:12:20.206
or CDC or just whatever,
legal people, whatever,

00:12:20.206 --> 00:12:22.208
just saying, hey,
you can stay open,

00:12:22.208 --> 00:12:24.977 align:start size:97% position:3%
but we're coming for you
basically one way or another.

00:12:24.977 --> 00:12:26.512
- Yeah. I think we got pushed.

00:12:26.512 --> 00:12:29.048 align:start size:97% position:3%
I mean, we got push back
from every angle, everywhere.

00:12:29.048 --> 00:12:31.417
And even the national
media made it worse

00:12:31.417 --> 00:12:33.886 align:start size:94% position:6%
because they just
specifically targeted me.

00:12:33.886 --> 00:12:35.755
There would be hotspots
all over the country,

00:12:35.755 --> 00:12:38.491 align:start size:94% position:6%
but all they talked about
at night on the news

00:12:38.491 --> 00:12:40.760
was South Dakota and
our meat packing plant

00:12:40.760 --> 00:12:42.628 align:start size:84% position:16%
or the Sturgis
Motorcycle Bike Rally,

00:12:42.628 --> 00:12:45.030
or this irresponsible event,

00:12:45.030 --> 00:12:47.233 align:start size:94% position:6%
we were going to be
hosting at Mount Rushmore.

00:12:47.233 --> 00:12:50.236 align:start size:97% position:3%
You know, they specifically
went after me in a way

00:12:50.236 --> 00:12:51.637
that we'd never seen before.

00:12:51.637 --> 00:12:56.475 align:start size:97% position:3%
And we saw the CDC
officials and NIH and Fauci,

00:12:56.475 --> 00:12:59.678 align:start size:97% position:3%
specifically attack me
along with Elizabeth Warren

00:12:59.678 --> 00:13:01.380
and Rachel Maddow,
night after night.

00:13:01.380 --> 00:13:05.050 align:start size:94% position:6%
But we also, you know,
recognized too that people

00:13:05.050 --> 00:13:08.554 align:start size:97% position:3%
in my state weren't
necessarily supportive either.

00:13:08.554 --> 00:13:10.890
You know, there was times
where it was hard to find

00:13:10.890 --> 00:13:13.692
a lot of encouragement, even
from people who lived here.

00:13:13.692 --> 00:13:15.928 align:start size:88% position:13%
That my Lieutenant
governor tells the story

00:13:15.928 --> 00:13:19.198
that he would get phone calls
from even our best supporters,

00:13:19.198 --> 00:13:21.700 align:start size:97% position:3%
that would say, hey, you gotta
get the governor in line.

00:13:21.700 --> 00:13:24.670 align:start size:97% position:3%
This is gonna, this is
gonna kill her politically.

00:13:24.670 --> 00:13:26.172 align:start size:84% position:16%
And she's gotta
start falling in line

00:13:26.172 --> 00:13:28.140 align:start size:88% position:13%
with these other
governors and you know,

00:13:28.140 --> 00:13:31.143
to his credit and to
my executive team,

00:13:31.143 --> 00:13:34.647 align:start size:84% position:16%
they said she is
firm in her decision.

00:13:34.647 --> 00:13:36.382
She has already decided this.

00:13:36.382 --> 00:13:37.583
She doesn't have the authority,

00:13:37.583 --> 00:13:39.819 align:start size:84% position:16%
therefore she's
not gonna overstep.

00:13:39.819 --> 00:13:42.688
And we are in line with her.

00:13:42.688 --> 00:13:46.425 align:start size:84% position:16%
And that made it a
wonderful thing for me

00:13:46.425 --> 00:13:48.527
because many times those
contentious phone calls

00:13:48.527 --> 00:13:50.362 align:start size:84% position:16%
they were taking, they
didn't necessarily

00:13:50.362 --> 00:13:52.498
always tell me about,
they knew where I was

00:13:52.498 --> 00:13:55.835
and we were a team taking
care of people together.

00:13:55.835 --> 00:13:58.070 align:start size:97% position:3%
- Did you ever have a moment
where you almost cracked,

00:13:58.070 --> 00:14:00.573
and said okay, I'm gonna
have to do some lockdowns

00:14:00.573 --> 00:14:02.575 align:start size:97% position:3%
or some version of something,
like did the pressure

00:14:02.575 --> 00:14:05.110
ever get to that point
where you got close?

00:14:05.110 --> 00:14:06.879 align:start size:88% position:13%
- You know, I don't
think I ever got close,

00:14:06.879 --> 00:14:08.347
although it was hard.

00:14:08.347 --> 00:14:11.383 align:start size:94% position:6%
There were days that, that
I would say, you know,

00:14:11.383 --> 00:14:14.286
I hung up a phone or got off
a zoom call where I thought,

00:14:14.286 --> 00:14:17.656 align:start size:94% position:6%
man, I don't know how
much longer I can do this.

00:14:17.656 --> 00:14:19.491 align:start size:84% position:16%
You know, my family
was pretty remarkable.

00:14:19.491 --> 00:14:23.963
I have a son that's now 20,
but he was 18 at the time

00:14:23.963 --> 00:14:28.968 align:start position:0%
that sat by my desk for 18
hours a day and just sat there.

00:14:30.102 --> 00:14:32.404
So, and I think it
was to remind me that

00:14:32.404 --> 00:14:34.673
somebody was gonna love me
no matter what, you know.

00:14:34.673 --> 00:14:38.344 align:start position:0%
And then when I got off a phone
call that was contentious,

00:14:38.344 --> 00:14:40.779 align:start size:97% position:3%
cause I spent a lot of time
managing other leaders.

00:14:40.779 --> 00:14:42.748
I spent a lot of time, you know,

00:14:42.748 --> 00:14:45.017
having conference
calls with mayors,

00:14:45.017 --> 00:14:49.154
with city council members,
with county commissioners.

00:14:49.154 --> 00:14:50.456
You know, once I
made a decision,

00:14:50.456 --> 00:14:53.859
I spent a ton of time
communicating with them

00:14:53.859 --> 00:14:56.662 align:start size:94% position:6%
about why I was making
the decision I was making.

00:14:56.662 --> 00:14:58.831
But when I would hang
up with a phone call

00:14:58.831 --> 00:15:03.435 align:start size:97% position:3%
that obviously was contentious
or stressful in a way,

00:15:03.435 --> 00:15:07.706 align:start position:0%
he would say, mom, let's go
shoot baskets for five minutes.

00:15:07.706 --> 00:15:11.310 align:start size:84% position:16%
And so we'd go outside
and shoot baskets

00:15:11.310 --> 00:15:13.145 align:start size:84% position:16%
for five or 10
minutes and, you know,

00:15:14.113 --> 00:15:15.214
get a little bit of a break

00:15:15.214 --> 00:15:16.482
and then I'd go back to work.

00:15:16.482 --> 00:15:18.384 align:start size:84% position:16%
Or let's go play
cards, but he was good

00:15:18.384 --> 00:15:20.619
at giving me some perspective.

00:15:20.619 --> 00:15:22.721
And I think my whole family,
you know, being here with me,

00:15:22.721 --> 00:15:24.156
made a world of difference.

00:15:24.156 --> 00:15:28.093 align:start size:97% position:3%
I mean, they really supported
me and reminded me that,

00:15:28.093 --> 00:15:31.630
here in South Dakota, just
because the national leaders

00:15:31.630 --> 00:15:34.566
are making very different
decisions didn't mean that

00:15:34.566 --> 00:15:36.135 align:start size:84% position:16%
that was gonna be
South Dakota's path.

00:15:36.135 --> 00:15:39.605
What I appreciated the most
was that President Trump

00:15:39.605 --> 00:15:43.075
let me do my job, is
if Joe Biden had been

00:15:43.075 --> 00:15:44.576
in the White House
during this time,

00:15:44.576 --> 00:15:47.146
I know for a fact he
would've come after us

00:15:47.146 --> 00:15:50.349 align:start position:0%
and really been punitive against
the state of South Dakota

00:15:50.349 --> 00:15:52.484 align:start size:84% position:16%
and done all he
could to destroy me.

00:15:52.484 --> 00:15:55.087
And President Trump let
me do my job as governor.

00:15:55.087 --> 00:15:56.922
And for that, I'll
always be grateful.

00:15:56.922 --> 00:15:59.158 align:start size:84% position:16%
- Are you shocked
or were you shocked,

00:15:59.158 --> 00:16:01.327
that during all of
this, the same people

00:16:01.327 --> 00:16:03.295 align:start size:88% position:13%
who were calling
Trump, Hitler every day

00:16:03.295 --> 00:16:05.064
and all the horrible things
that they would call him,

00:16:05.064 --> 00:16:08.167
that he was basically kicking
the power back to the states.

00:16:08.167 --> 00:16:10.436 align:start size:81% position:19%
So taking less
power for himself,

00:16:10.436 --> 00:16:12.538
less power to the
federal government.

00:16:12.538 --> 00:16:14.573
And they were angry
at him for that.

00:16:14.573 --> 00:16:16.976
The guy who they said was
the worst of the worst.

00:16:16.976 --> 00:16:19.044
- That's right. Yeah.
That's exactly it.

00:16:19.044 --> 00:16:22.915 align:start size:88% position:13%
And what I found was
even at the local level,

00:16:22.915 --> 00:16:24.950 align:start size:84% position:16%
many mayors and city
council members,

00:16:24.950 --> 00:16:26.685 align:start size:84% position:16%
they wanted me to make
the tough decision

00:16:26.685 --> 00:16:28.253
so they didn't have to.

00:16:28.253 --> 00:16:31.724
And it was so, that's the exact
opposite of President Trump.

00:16:31.724 --> 00:16:33.926 align:start position:0%
It wasn't that he wasn't
willing to make tough decisions.

00:16:33.926 --> 00:16:36.962
He was very clear on what
his recommendations were,

00:16:36.962 --> 00:16:38.664
but then he let us do our job.

00:16:38.664 --> 00:16:40.733
And that's what I kept
saying to the mayors.

00:16:40.733 --> 00:16:42.334 align:start size:84% position:16%
And the city
councilman is listen,

00:16:42.334 --> 00:16:44.570 align:start size:84% position:16%
this is not my
authority to do this.

00:16:44.570 --> 00:16:46.638 align:start size:84% position:16%
You can make
different decisions,

00:16:46.638 --> 00:16:48.173
but I want you to know
that I'm gonna be here

00:16:48.173 --> 00:16:49.808
holding you accountable as well.

00:16:49.808 --> 00:16:54.113
If you shut down your city and
we have a hundred cases there

00:16:54.113 --> 00:16:56.515
and then two weeks from
now, you open it back up,

00:16:56.515 --> 00:16:57.916
cause people can't
take it anymore.

00:16:57.916 --> 00:17:00.486
And you have 500 cases.
I'm gonna blow you up.

00:17:00.486 --> 00:17:02.187 align:start size:84% position:16%
I'm gonna call a press
conference and say,

00:17:02.187 --> 00:17:04.223
you locked people in their homes

00:17:04.223 --> 00:17:07.026
and only let them out
when it got worse.

00:17:07.026 --> 00:17:10.362 align:start size:94% position:6%
So I said, I consistently
was reminding people,

00:17:10.362 --> 00:17:12.965
why don't you shut off your TV?

00:17:12.965 --> 00:17:16.035 align:start size:97% position:3%
Let's talk over the data
and the facts of this virus.

00:17:16.035 --> 00:17:18.370 align:start size:88% position:13%
And then just, I'll
call you in the morning.

00:17:18.370 --> 00:17:21.106
I know you're nervous.
I know you're anxious.

00:17:21.106 --> 00:17:22.608
I know you're uncertain.

00:17:22.608 --> 00:17:24.643
So let's just take a night and
I'll call you in the morning

00:17:24.643 --> 00:17:26.145
and let's talk again.

00:17:26.145 --> 00:17:29.448 align:start size:94% position:6%
And a lot of what happened
during the pandemic was

00:17:29.448 --> 00:17:32.951 align:start position:0%
that kind of communication that
I think was to our benefit.

00:17:32.951 --> 00:17:34.987
- I would be remiss if I
didn't back up for a second

00:17:34.987 --> 00:17:36.522 align:start size:84% position:16%
and ask you about your
basketball skills.

00:17:36.522 --> 00:17:38.157
Do you have some
skills out there?

00:17:38.157 --> 00:17:39.792
- They needed some tuning up.

00:17:39.792 --> 00:17:42.628 align:start size:88% position:13%
So they were not
horrible in high school.

00:17:42.628 --> 00:17:46.198
I did play basketball, but
yeah, that 6'5" son of mine,

00:17:46.198 --> 00:17:49.435
can school me every day
on the basketball court.

00:17:49.435 --> 00:17:51.336
- So speaking of
sports, actually,

00:17:51.336 --> 00:17:52.604
we got a good transition there.

00:17:52.604 --> 00:17:54.506
One of the other things
you've been leading on

00:17:54.506 --> 00:17:58.210 align:start size:84% position:16%
is actually keeping
boys and girls

00:17:58.210 --> 00:18:00.979 align:start size:84% position:16%
in separate sports
leagues and it's okay.

00:18:00.979 --> 00:18:03.849 align:start size:94% position:6%
And it's not based in
bigotry or anything else.

00:18:03.849 --> 00:18:06.985 align:start size:94% position:6%
Are you shocked that this
even got to your desk

00:18:06.985 --> 00:18:09.121
and that you had to kind
of lead on this thing,

00:18:09.121 --> 00:18:10.722 align:start size:84% position:16%
cause everyone's
talking about it now,

00:18:10.722 --> 00:18:12.191
but you were really doing this.

00:18:12.191 --> 00:18:14.426
It's almost two years
ago at this point.

00:18:14.426 --> 00:18:17.696 align:start size:97% position:3%
- You know, I started this
fight probably five years ago.

00:18:17.696 --> 00:18:19.131 align:start size:84% position:16%
You know, people
don't remember this,

00:18:19.131 --> 00:18:20.632
but when I was in Congress,

00:18:20.632 --> 00:18:23.569
the federal government came
down and told South Dakota

00:18:23.569 --> 00:18:26.738
that we could no longer have
girls events and boys events

00:18:26.738 --> 00:18:28.941 align:start size:84% position:16%
in the sport of rodeo,
which, you know,

00:18:28.941 --> 00:18:30.542
a lot of people don't
pay attention to rodeo.

00:18:30.542 --> 00:18:32.277
So it never made national news.

00:18:32.277 --> 00:18:34.279
But to South Dakota, that
was a really big deal.

00:18:34.279 --> 00:18:35.948
It's our state sport.

00:18:35.948 --> 00:18:38.717
And they told us that it
was too discriminatory

00:18:38.717 --> 00:18:41.019 align:start size:84% position:16%
to have a girls' event
and a boys' event.

00:18:41.019 --> 00:18:43.422 align:start size:97% position:3%
And that we had to eliminate
all gender references

00:18:43.422 --> 00:18:44.990
in that sport statewide.

00:18:44.990 --> 00:18:48.393
And I fought them.
I fought them alone.

00:18:48.393 --> 00:18:51.396 align:start size:94% position:6%
Nobody really cared at
that time about this issue

00:18:51.396 --> 00:18:53.999
or they thought it was
too politically dangerous

00:18:53.999 --> 00:18:56.735
to really wade into,
but ended up winning

00:18:56.735 --> 00:18:58.003
that argument at that time.

00:18:58.003 --> 00:19:00.506
So this is something I'd
been a part of fighting

00:19:00.506 --> 00:19:02.074 align:start size:81% position:19%
for many, many
years when it came

00:19:02.074 --> 00:19:03.408
to protecting girls sports.

00:19:03.408 --> 00:19:05.377
Now that it's a national issue.

00:19:05.377 --> 00:19:08.313 align:start size:84% position:16%
I wanted the strongest
bill in the nation,

00:19:08.313 --> 00:19:09.815
in the state of South Dakota.

00:19:09.815 --> 00:19:13.085
Because I know that this
is a basic fairness issue.

00:19:13.085 --> 00:19:16.155
This is about only girls
playing in girls sports.

00:19:16.155 --> 00:19:18.023 align:start size:97% position:3%
So they have an opportunity
to be successful

00:19:18.023 --> 00:19:20.559
and to succeed, maybe
win a scholarship,

00:19:20.559 --> 00:19:22.528
go on and play professionally,

00:19:22.528 --> 00:19:24.363
if that opportunity
presents itself.

00:19:24.363 --> 00:19:26.565 align:start size:94% position:6%
And that biological males
should not be playing

00:19:26.565 --> 00:19:28.066
in their same events.

